1. Absolute Path vs Relative Path

Absolute Path
Starts from root directory
Example: C:/Users/hp/test or /c/Users/hp/test
Independent of current directory
Always points to the same location


Relative Path
Starts from current directory
Example: sub/, ../test
Depends on current working directory
Changes meaning if location changes

2. Directory Creation (mkdir)
mkdir test → creates a directory
mkdir test_sub_folder → underscore allowed
mkdir "test sub folder" → spaces require quotes
mkdir test sub folder → creates multiple folders (test, sub, folder)
Important
Folder names are case-sensitive
Error shown if folder already exists


3. Listing Files (ls)
ls → normal list
ls -a → shows hidden files (starting with .)
ls -i → shows inode numbers (unique file identifiers)


4. Navigation (cd)
cd foldername → move into folder
cd .. → move one level up
cd ~ → move to home directory
cd - → go to previous directory


5. Nested Directories
mkdir -p level1/level2/level3/level4
Creates full structure in one command
No error if directories already exist


6. Removing Directories (rm)
rm -ri level1
-r → recursive delete
-i → asks confirmation before deleting
Safest way to delete directories


7. Files Creation (touch)
touch env → normal file
touch .env → hidden file
touch random1.txt random2.txt → multiple files


8. Hidden Files
Files starting with . are hidden
Example: .env
Visible only with ls -a


9. Inode Numbers
Each file/folder has a unique inode
Seen using ls -i
File name can change, inode stays same


10. Key Takeaways
Use absolute paths for scripts and configs
Use relative paths for portability
Quotes are mandatory for spaces in names
mkdir -p saves time for deep structures


